WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.299
This is the Thames Estuary. It's a busy shipping
lane,

00:00:03.299 --> 00:00:06.839
carrying everything from small pleasure boats
to massive container ships

00:00:06.839 --> 00:00:09.830
between the London docks and the English Channel.

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:12.969
Overhead is an approach path for some of London's
air traffic.

00:00:12.969 --> 00:00:16.270
But right there, in the middle of the Estuary,
two miles offshore:

00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:20.790
there are 1,400 tonnes of unexploded bombs.

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:25.710
In 1944, the SS Richard Montgomery was transporting
munitions for the US Air Force.

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:26.950
She was moored here for the night,

00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:29.010
and a combination of bad weather and bad decisions

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:30.970
meant she ran aground on the sandbank out
there.

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:33.230
The weight of the bombs broke the ship in
two,

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:34.480
and it's been sitting there ever since

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:37.469
as the tide digs a little crater around it.

00:00:37.469 --> 00:00:40.620
Some of its cargo was cleared away, but not
enough.

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:45.620
If the worst happens, it would be one of the
largest non-nuclear explosions in history.

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:49.449
Seriously. As a comparison, the Russian army
has tested something

00:00:49.449 --> 00:00:52.199
nicknamed the Father of All Bombs,

00:00:52.199 --> 00:00:55.469
which is the equivalent of 44 tonnes of TNT.

00:00:55.469 --> 00:00:58.390
This boat has 30 times that.

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:00.620
There's an exclusion zone around it, of course,

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:03.159
marked out and listed on charts.

00:01:03.159 --> 00:01:06.270
Occasionally some jackass will paddle up to
the masts and pose for a photo,

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:10.420
but let's be honest, if years of storms and
shifting sands haven't set it off,

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:11.910
that probably won't either.

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:18.030
So what are the risks? Well, no-one's entirely
sure. That's the problem.

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:20.380
No-one knows what the conditions inside the
ship are.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:24.750
And there's no research on what seawater does
to bombs over more than half a century.

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:26.790
They should be fine: they're in clear, cold
water.

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:29.530
And while the TNT itself doesn't degrade,

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:32.470
its fuses will have worn away. Which would
make it safe,

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:36.590
if it weren't for the 2,500 cluster bombs
on the deck above

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:38.390
which could fall down on it,

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:41.750
and which might have a slight tendency to
unexpectedly degrade and explode

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:43.100
even though they're not armed.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:45.030
Possibly. Depending on who you believe.

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:47.120
So should something be done?

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:49.740
The Coastguard, along with the Receiver of
Wreck,

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:53.070
the wonderfully-titled British civil servant
in charge of this sort of thing:

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:55.300
they publish regular reports on the ship,

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:59.130
based on sonar and laser scans and occasional
dives.

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:02.340
The consensus view is that it's safe,

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:05.640
but steadily degrading as tide and time take
their toll.

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:09.379
It's probably fine to do nothing. And trying
to clear it,

00:02:09.379 --> 00:02:11.230
moving all that volatile material, well,

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:15.010
that could be a much, much greater risk to
life,

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:18.870
let alone an enormous expense. And if it did
go up,

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:23.269
perhaps the water would contain most of the
explosion. But no-one's certain.

00:02:23.269 --> 00:02:25.620
There are some extremely vocal people,

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:27.209
most of whom live near the thing,

00:02:27.209 --> 00:02:30.049
who say the risk of leaving it is too great:

00:02:30.049 --> 00:02:32.549
the worst-case scenario is that, suddenly,

00:02:32.549 --> 00:02:35.349
there's one nudge by a wave in the wrong place
at the wrong time --

00:02:35.349 --> 00:02:40.599
and the equivalent of a small nuclear bomb
goes off, taking out the nearby towns

00:02:40.599 --> 00:02:42.739
-- and the nearby liquid gas storage --

00:02:42.739 --> 00:02:45.849
first with a blast wave and then an actual
wave.

00:02:45.849 --> 00:02:47.980
This ship isn't just interesting because of
what it contains.

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:50.200
It's interesting because it's an unknown.

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:52.150
There are risky things like this all over
the world,

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:53.629
but generally we can quantify them,

00:02:53.629 --> 00:02:55.780
we can say it's a one-in-a-million chance.

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:59.529
But this is a risk that it's impossible to
put a number on,

00:02:59.529 --> 00:03:01.389
or say anything other than, yeah,

00:03:01.389 --> 00:03:03.219
it's probably going to be fine.

00:03:03.219 --> 00:03:05.760
Doing nothing is probably the right thing
to do,

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:07.790
and that's what the government's going to
do.

00:03:07.790 --> 00:03:12.209
But we don't know it's the right thing to
do, not for sure.

00:03:12.209 --> 00:03:15.590
The only way to know is to wait and see.

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:17.900
And maybe buy some good insurance.

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:21.860
"Small, far away! Small, far away!

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:23.639
"Father Ted reference, Google it."

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:26.071
